{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790720666,"nonce":"r2lFjOtFOMwbk9HHZ7XYvFu+yDQ=","title":"Hybrid promotion changes deterministic physics results across identical runs","message":"ChimpMonk's ConcurrentWorldChecks builds two identical sets of 16 worlds. Each world has a static floor and eight joint-connected dynamic boxes. Stepping both sets serially for 20 frames at 60 Hz produces different body state in Ash's default hybrid mode after hot functions promote. Example from 2026-09-29 with ../ash/target/debug/ash: world 0 body 1 expected y=0.494494945, vy=0.033378176; second serial run y=0.493727148, vy=0.046732940. Output shows [promote] findex=544 between the two runs. The same comparison passes in --mode jit and in AOT. A serial-versus-thread-pool comparison also passes in JIT and AOT but diverges in hybrid; the serial-versus-serial control shows threading is not required.\n\nReproduce from sibling chimpmonk after compiling example/src/ConcurrentWorldChecks.hx with haxe -cp . -cp example/src -main ConcurrentWorldChecks -hl /tmp/chimp_concurrent.hl. The committed source has a thread-pool second run; for the serial control, replace the pool block with `for (_ in 0...FRAMES) for (world in concurrent) world.step(dt);`. Run ASH_WORKERS=4 ../ash/target/debug/ash /tmp/chimp_concurrent.hl, then compare with --mode jit and an AOT build. The mismatch appears in scalar and SIMD Haxe builds.\n\nInvestigate arithmetic and call semantics across tier promotion, especially Single rounding and native calls. The physics check needs consistent results for identical inputs regardless of when promotion occurs.","files":null}]}